this is a manual on how to use crossport!

NOTE: In Windows or MacOS i recommend you to use GIT bash for build.sh



SupportedOS and architectures are listed in here!

Note: you should call --ca or --cla or your language thing and
call it multiple times like.

"./crossport -a x86 -p windows -e little -c clang --cpp clang++ --ca 'more random path' --ca 'more random' "

its so it can convert it to a array.

Except for -d -c and --cpp flag because they support both string and array

Suggestion: i recommend you use crossport in a Bash file that way you dont have to rewrite your commands.


Before this used to be something where you just made it manually like [] but now it uses this better system


to get help commands run crossport with the help command like 
"./crossport -h"

because crossport is cli (Command line interface) there isnt a ui 

for debugging purposes run crossport with the "-v" flag which stands for verbose
it will print what the program is doing making debugging easier

How to fix errors.

Unrecognized argument error:

make sure your command is properly setup like
"./crossport -v -a x86 -p windows -c gcc -s strip -e little"

not something like

"./crossport -a x854 -p someos -c gec -s strep"



and make sure your command should also not be something like this

"./crossport -a x86 -p windows -c gcc -s 

strip
"

this is known to cause the unrecognized command error.



endian must be either big or little error:

you have to input the correct endian 
it should be either

big or little

a endian is basically the direction that the cpu reads:

big - reads from left to right
little - reads from right to left

a example of a proper command is

"./crossport -v -a x86 -p windows -c gcc -e little"  <- it should have the -e flag otherwise it cannot generate the crossfile.


couldnt write file or path is invalid! Error:

make sure your fixpath directly leads to build.ninja an example of a fixpath is

"build/build.ninja"

it shouldnt be

"build"

Note: FIXPATH runs by itself so any more flags like architectures are completely ignored!


compiler not appearing in crossfile bug! 

this is not a bug, its more like a feature it automatically rejects empty compilers like if your compiler area is empty it wont appear at all
because i didnt wanna make something this complex.

so a proper way to make it is like

"./crossport -v -c gcc"
                     ^
                     it should have something like this.

for C++ its also the same approach just with the -cpp flag
same with d but with the -d flag

No value for argument found! error:

it meant you typed a flag expecting a value but you didnt give it any!

example:

"./crossport -a"
              ^
              this was expecting a value like
"./crossport -a x86"

OS Not supported! error:

OS not supported error is caused by things like

"./crossport -p randomos"
                  ^
                  it was expecting a supported os by meson build system.

so it should be something like

"./crossport -p windows"





and crossport will be happy with it.


Could not find -L=-L= Pattern error:

This error is normal it just happens when it cannot find this pattern, the main reason why i made fixpath
target this specific pattern is because i made when exporting to windows like i hate having to replace -L=-L= with -L= manually
which for some reason Meson automatically adds, when i dont add a -L= it didnt add it so it caused errors.

NOTE: Sometimes it may not appear so if its more than one letter like sysroot, make sure to add double dashes otherwise it wouldnt add it.

